---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Why does your portfolio look plain?">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
    <section
      class="pt-14 pb-10 border-b-2"
      style="border-color: var(--color-border)"
    >
      <p
        class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest font-semibold text-text-muted mb-3"
      >
        Design Choice
      </p>
      <h1
        class="font-display font-black uppercase text-4xl md:text-5xl leading-tight tracking-tight"
        style="color: var(--color-text)"
      >
        Why does your portfolio<br />look like this?
      </h1>
    </section>

    <div class="pt-10 pb-16 max-w-2xl space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h2
          class="font-display font-bold uppercase text-xs tracking-widest mb-4"
          style="color: var(--color-text)"
        >
          Intentional
        </h2>
        <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Brutalist design strips everything back to what matters — structure,
          hierarchy, content. No gradients to hide weak ideas. No carousels to
          distract from bad work. What you see is what there is: bold type,
          strong borders, and the actual work.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2
          class="font-display font-bold uppercase text-xs tracking-widest mb-4"
          style="color: var(--color-text)"
        >
          Builder First
        </h2>
        <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
          I'm a builder. The most important thing on this site is what I'm
          building right now, and what I've built. The design reflects that
          priority — no decorative clutter between you and the work. If
          something doesn't help you understand who I am or what I build, it
          doesn't belong here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2
          class="font-display font-bold uppercase text-xs tracking-widest mb-4"
          style="color: var(--color-text)"
        >
          Copycats
        </h2>
        <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Inspiration drives creativity. I've refined my skills by learning
          from the work of others, and I welcome anyone who finds value in what
          I create. There's a clear line between drawing inspiration and
          outright copying. Blatantly replicating and claiming someone else's
          work as your own is unacceptable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
